Having all his thoughts, actions, and even what he held dear laid bare truly must have felt like a nightmare for Clind.

But, the feeling concerning nightmares was mutual. Subaru had been through his own personal hell more times than he could count.

Echidna: [Want me to tell you how many?]

Subaru: [I’ll pass.]

Rejecting his companion’s offer, who likely had been dutifully keeping track, Subaru focused his attention back on Clind.

Behind those piercing eyes of his, Clind was grappling with inner turmoil. Whatever that conflict was, unfortunately, it lay entirely within Subaru’s grasp.

Clind struggled between securing Annerose’s safety, and pursuing his own objective.

And, Subaru already knew which way the scales would tip.

Subaru: [So, what will it be? Will you surrender?]

Clind: [――No. Preserving the world takes priority. Disloyalty.]

Clind’s gaze sharpened as he said this, his focus shifting toward Subaru’s chest.

Subaru sensed the impending danger. He knew Clind’s Authority of unknown origins would, just like before, try to destroy his heart and inflict a fatal, unrecoverable wound.

Which was why Subaru dealt with it before it could reach him.

Subaru: [――THE WITCH CULT!!]

Clind: [――!?]

Clind’s eyes widened as Subaru’s voice, filled with sharp tension, resounded through the hall.

Right after that moment, just as Clind gasped in surprise.

――A group of fierce warriors, radiating an overwhelming swordsmanship, attacked Clind all at once.

△▼△▼△▼△

Preparing in advance had not been a difficult task for Subaru.

As had been repeatedly stated, Subaru was one of the main guests of honor at this evening’s commendation ceremony. Furthermore, his efforts in taking down the White Whale and Sloth had more than earned him the merit.

The idea that the Witch Cult had sent an assassin to the commendation ceremony to target Subaru was all too convincing to those who had recently clashed with the Cult.

Of course, there was some resistance to the suggestion that the assassin was none other than Clind, the long-serving steward of the Mathers family.

The one most strongly opposed to this was Frederica, who had been trained in the basics of service by Clind himself, and Ram also seemed to share her sentiments to some extent.

Therefore, Subaru had proposed one condition to convince them and those around him.

The condition was――,

Echidna: [――That Clind shows murderous intent toward you.]

Subaru: [Well, things might get dicey before anyone can make a move if I provoke him too much.]

If things went wrong, he could always just start over.

In actuality, even with Annerose as a hostage, Clind’s inner conflict still pushed him to choose duty over the safety of his master.

However, the full significance of that duty remained a mystery.

Subaru: [Trying to dig out the true motives of the type of man who doesn’t care for parting words is no easy task.]

If every enemy were to spill their plans to their dying foe, not many in this world would be compatible with this as much as Subaru. It would make planning for their next encounter much simpler.

But, in reality, most people met their end without hearing much from their killer.

Perhaps they rambled on to their corpses after death, but it would no longer be Subaru’s concern by that point.

In any case, Subaru had fulfilled all the necessary conditions to corner Clind.

Clind’s fatal mistake was falling for Subaru’s provocations and making his move on the spot. If he had taken a better look at his surroundings, he might have noticed that he was being watched.

Echidna: [But you secured his mistress to throw him off balance and keep him from noticing.]

The fabricated information about Clind being a Witch Cultist, the efforts to ensure he would not realize he was under suspicion, and a hostage to force his hand.

Subaru had gone through all this just to bring things to this anticipated conclusion.

If any of these conditions had not been met, Subaru would have fallen prey to Clind’s Authority for months and years on end―― counting all the attempts required to identify him as the enemy, Subaru’s retries easily reached four figures.

But, all that time and effort had paid off.

Subaru: [You tried to kill me right in front of everyone. There’s no explaining your way out of that.]

Indeed, security around the ceremony had intensified, and the evacuation of key figures was swiftly underway.

Seeing the situation turning against him, Clind had leaped from the balcony. However, in light of his previous actions, it was hard to believe he intended to simply flee.

Left unchecked, who knew what Clind might attempt next? Subaru was not the only one who thought so.

And if Clind was thinking the same, then the only logical conclusion was that tonight, here and now, would be the final showdown.

Subaru: [――Hey hey, where are you, bro! Playing hide and seek? How cold!]

Leaning over the balcony railing, Subaru called out provocatively into the garden below.

If he could not pinpoint Clind’s location, he would just make himself a grand, obvious target. Anyone in Clind’s position would feel compelled to take a shot at Subaru, seeing him standing there wide open and defenseless.

Echidna: [And if that ends up getting you killed?]

Subaru: [So long as I can figure out the trick behind Clind’s Authority, I’ll call it a win even if he crushes my heart!]

So far, the only thing Subaru knew was that Clind had managed to crush his heart from a distance.

It was an instant-death attack, but it could not be something he could use without any limits.

The number of uses, continuity, and activation conditions. Every time Subaru learned one more detail, he inched closer to putting Clind in checkmate.

Subaru: [Wilhelm-san, Julius, and even the captain of the Royal Guard are with me. I’m sorry it’s so one-sided, but I’m gonna crush you with everything I have at my disposal.]

Echidna: [You sure are talking a lot… Is this one of your Days of Joy?]

Subaru: [Is that so? I wasn’t aware of that though.]

Responding to the voice of Echidna in his head, Subaru bared his teeth as he searched for his elusive enemy.

As Echidna had called it, a Day of Joy was one of Subaru’s mental states, alongside his Days of Wrath and Calm. It seemed to describe the emotional ups and downs he experienced.

Compared to the other two, Subaru did not show any particularly subjective symptoms. Unlike becoming violently aggressive or sinking into despair, it did not seem like a problem.

Therefore――,

Subaru: [I’ve given you company till now. Stop running around like a mouse, and come at me from the front!]

He deliberately raised his voice, broadcasting his position, and acted provocatively, hoping to flush out his opponent.

Everything was part of the plan. There were no issues there.

Perhaps he was not exactly calm, but one could say he was in the zone. Riding this momentum would lead to a good outcome. Even though he could die as many times as needed, in this situation, he felt like he had died a little too much. Getting reckless with his lives was not the best idea.

Therefore, he judged that taking appropriate actions to secure victory was essential.

???: [――OHHH!]

Suddenly, a loud noise sounded from behind the venue.

It was likely that Clind had clashed with one of his pursuers. Subaru hurried toward the commotion, eager to join the fray.

It was not just his curiosity, influenced by Echidna’s penchant for spectacle. No, it was crucial for Subaru to witness the facts with his own eyes.

――Otherwise, he would not fully grasp Clind’s actions for the next round.

Subaru: [Tsk.]

Rounding the back of the building, Subaru clicked his tongue at the sight that greeted him.

The neatly trimmed hedges and vibrant backyard flower beds were now marred by the bodies of those who had fought valiantly against Clind. They lay crumpled, utterly defeated.

Among them were familiar faces, and Subaru scratched his head at the realization.

Subaru: [I thought I knew this already, but the troublesomeness of Authority holders really is too much to handle.]

Clind: […Is that all you have to say? Heartless.]

Subaru: [Heartless? That’s rich coming from the guy responsible for all this mess.]

Without exception, every fallen figure had their life force entirely extinguished.

Whether it was out of courtesy or caution, Clind had shown no mercy. Either way, the scene was so gruesome that one would want to cover their eyes.

It would have been enough to leave him broken beyond repair had this situation not been reversible.

Subaru: [But, you’re not unscathed either. So, who gave you the most trouble? You’d be doing me a favor by letting me know for future reference.]

Clind: [――! Your acquaintances got rolled up into this and died, and yet this is all you care about? There is too much that is crooked about your way of being. Hideous.]

Clind’s lips curled as he spoke, his voice filled with emotion, his body covered in countless wounds.

He had faced numerous skilled opponents, some of whom even Subaru had relied on as top-tier fighters. Naturally, Clind had not escaped unscathed.

His tattered coat lay discarded, his shirt soaked in blood from a gash across his chest. His monocle lay shattered on the grass, exposing his sharp features as he glared at the defenseless Subaru.

His eyes were filled with fierce hatred and righteous indignation, as if cursing Subaru for being inhuman.

Subaru: [It seems like our opinions don’t match. Guess this is the first time I understand how Echidna feels.]

Echidna: [Maybe now you will treat me a little better.]

Subaru: [I’ll think about it.]

Clind: [Natsuki Subaru-sama, it looks like we’re both already not compatible with each other… Resolve.]

Ignoring his side exchange with Echidna, Clind’s eyes were locked on Subaru, full of unwavering hostility.

That hostility was about to take shape and strike Subaru down―― right before something intervened.

Frederica: [CLIIIIIIIIND――!!]

A gust of wind tore between Subaru and Clind, accompanied by the flash of long golden hair and the imposing figure of a tall woman clad in a maid’s uniform.

Her emerald eyes welled with tears, and her sharp fangs were bared in fury. It was Frederica.

At the sight of her, Clind’s expression stiffened ever so slightly.

Clind: [Frederica…]

Frederica: [I always looked down on you, Clind. But regardless of what, I never expected you to get yourself involved with something so tyrannical…]

Grinding her teeth, Frederica surveyed the carnage around her, voicing her bitter frustration.

The blood-soaked garden Clind had created was littered with far more than just ten or twenty lost lives, and it utterly shattered the trust Frederica had held in him, trust that had held strong till the very end, into tiny pieces.

She knew the option of clearing up doubts through discussion was off the table, for it had been Clind who had first shown intent to kill Subaru.

Thus, Frederica glared at Clind with rage and sorrow in her eyes.

Frederica: [Why did you stray from the right path, Clind?]

Clind: [.…No matter how much you say, nothing will change. Pointless.]

Frederica: [――! Whether it is meaningless or not is not for you to decide, it’s up to us!]

Clind: [――. I’m sorry. Apologies.]

As Frederica let out a fierce roar, Clind lowered his gaze and uttered those words.

In that moment, Subaru, though an outsider, could almost hear the sound of Frederica’s heart cracking.

Frederica: [――――]

Frederica’s pupils narrowed, and a low growl rumbled from her throat.

In the blink of an eye, her body began to transform, white skin covered by golden fur as her bones audibly shifted. Frederica’s form grew in size at an incredible speed.

The next moment, a female golden leopard stood in her place.

The maid’s uniform was shredded apart as she transformed, clinging like tattered remnants to her hulking frame as the beast-like Frederica crouched low.

And then――,

Clind: [No matter how many years have passed, you never change. Childlike innocence.]

Frederica: [How dare you… blown away!!]

With a swift leap, Frederica’s body became a whirlwind, the bloodstained petals around her dancing in the violent gust.

As she charged forward at terrifying speed, she caught Clind off guard, sending his body flying into the air. Blood spurted from his left shoulder as he spun, and before he could regain his balance, more savage strikes rained down upon him from all sides.

Echidna: [Tremendous.]

As Echidna’s brief remark suggested, Frederica’s speed was nothing short of extraordinary.

Her movements had always been impressive in her beast form, but the anger she channeled through her limbs after being betrayed had propelled her speed to an entirely new level.

Her claws and fangs tore into Clind’s flesh, slowly but surely sapping his remaining strength.

Yet, the fact that Frederica’s power could surpass that of the Knights, who had already fallen in the garden, was unlikely. Her fangs only found their mark because those who had fought before her had already weakened Clind’s reserves and, perhaps, because of the emotional strain Clind was under.

Even someone as composed as Clind would struggle when faced with turning an old acquaintance into an enemy.

Subaru: [Maybe they’re more than just old acquaintances.]

Echidna: [――? What do you mean by that?]

Subaru: [It’d be hard to explain the intricacies of male-female relationships to you, so I’ll pass.]

Although they both saw the same thing, Subaru ignored the Witch and her inability to grasp the situation.

The battle between Frederica and Clind was reaching its climax before his eyes.

Clind: [――――]

Still suspended in midair, Clind was helpless as he took the full brunt of Frederica’s attacks.

The wounds he sustained and the amount of blood he was losing were accelerating, pushing him ever closer to death from blood loss. However, even with death looming, Clind’s eyes remained undaunted.

Amidst the relentless onslaught, his gaze was firmly locked on Subaru.

As if Frederica was nothing more than an afterthought to him.

Frederica: [Where on earth are you looking at――!]

That realization seemed to dawn on Frederica as well.

Her voice trembled with frustration at being ignored, and to Subaru, it almost sounded like she was on the verge of tears.

It was the anguished cry of a young girl, desperately pleading with an adult for attention, unable to comprehend why he would not take her seriously. Her pained voice struck Subaru deeply, but the cold-hearted man remained unaffected.

Without shifting his gaze from Subaru, Clind reached out his hands and said,

Clind: [This too, is for the world… no, for the promise I made. Impoliteness.]

With a voice tinged with regret, Clind’s hands clenched.

In the next instant, Frederica, who had been charging across the ground, lost her balance and was violently thrown to the ground.

Frederica: [――hk!?]

Frederica, covered in dust, had no idea what had just happened.

All she knew was that her limbs had been crushed, and she had lost the ability to run across the ground. And――,

Frederica: [S-Subaru-sama…! Please, you must flee!]

The path to Subaru was wide open, with nothing and no one standing in the way.

Frederica: [――――]

Writhing on the ground, Frederica’s body slowly began reverting from beast to human. But, of course, the wounds she had sustained did not conveniently heal in the process.

With her limbs crushed and her body covered in grievous injuries, Frederica lay there helpless. And yet, despite being horribly wounded himself, Clind walked past her with steady steps.

Clind’s body was as battered and bloodied as Frederica’s, but his eyes showed no sign of weakness. Only an unwavering, ironclad resolve.

Subaru: [So you can make that kind of face too. How unexpected, Clind-san.]

Clind: […The words I have exchanged with you are far too brief. Scant.]

Subaru: [Yeah, you’re right. I wanted to talk more to you…. It’s sorta got a deceptive tone about it, but that’s what I really think. Perhaps we could have built a different relationship.]

In another timeline, in a what-if scenario, perhaps there could have been a different path.

But even if that path had existed, becoming closer to someone destined to betray him would only have hurt Subaru more.

Subaru: [That’s why I am glad I didn’t become friends with you. This is the right path]

Clind: [――. You really are acting complacent. No one can defend you anymore. Defenseless.]

Clind frowned in suspicion as Subaru casually stood there with his hands in his pockets.

Subaru understood why he was confused, and part of him felt slightly guilty. But, after enduring countless Deaths, this was only natural.

Subaru: [Sorry, but you won’t shake me so easily anymore.]

Clind: [――――?]

Subaru: [I’m not feigning confidence. If you wanted to, Clind-san, you could kill me in an instant. And that’s not a big deal.]

If he were to die, he would just come back with a new plan.

Echidna and Roswaal understood why Subaru’s Authority was the most terrifying, yet the most remarkable. Even Subaru knew this was essentially cheating.

He was using his cheat ability to the fullest, as one would expect of someone who had been summoned to another world.

Clind: [Your thinking is hard to comprehend. Unfathomable. But, prepare yourself for――]

Subaru: [Oh, just one more thing.]

Clind had given up on trying to understand Subaru’s strange confidence.

But, before his hands could reach Subaru’s chest, Subaru interrupted him, raising a single finger as he pulled his hand from his pocket,

Subaru: [――I made sure Frederica would be next if I died.]

Clind: [――――]

Subaru didn’t miss the slight twitch that froze Clind’s expression.

That reaction alone was more than enough of a gain.

Clind’s weak point was neither his Contract with Roswaal, nor was it Annerose.

Clind: [The real threat was never the Witch. It was you. Wicked.]

Subaru heard Clind mutter in a trembling voice, horrified to the core by the realization.

Calling him wicked might have been a bit much, Subaru thought, feeling dissatisfied as the moment his heart would burst was approaching――,

△▼△▼△▼△

???: [It was an unknown situation, but you handled it rather well. How are you feeling?]

Subaru: [Nothing to worry about. I’m feeling great.]

On the familiar hill, Subaru responded to Echidna’s straightforward question.

Subaru was dressed in the black formal attire he had worn for the commendation ceremony. It might be a bit late to mention this, but the dress code for the Witch’s tea party was whatever the guest happened to be wearing when they were summoned.

As a result, he had been stuck in his tracksuit during the tea parties in the Sanctuary, and this time, he was stuck in his formal attire.

Subaru: [Let’s just hope I don’t get accidentally summoned while I’m in the bathroom.]

Echidna: [Ah, that’s a way to do it.]

Subaru: [That wasn’t a suggestion. You’re more of a peeping Witch than a curious bystander.]

Echidna snapped her fingers with a serious look on her face, and Subaru, leaning in, flicked her on the forehead. “Ow!” the Witch exclaimed, recoiling with a grimace.

Echidna rubbed her reddened forehead and said,

Echidna: [How rude of you. Not like by now there isn’t a place on your body that I haven’t seen.]

Subaru: [Yeah, that’s because I bring that Magic Crystal everywhere, even in the toilet and the bath! But, it’s about my feelings, okay? Modesty is important. Absolutely.]

Echidna: [You have the values of a maiden…]

Echidna muttered in exasperation, lowering her hand from her forehead, where the redness had already faded from her alabaster skin. Subaru could not help but be impressed by the Witch’s flair for theatrics.

In any case, as Echidna had pointed out, the unknown situation had been handled.

Subaru: [Identifying the enemy, assessing the situation, establishing countermeasures, and finding the best solution…]

Echidna: [That fight revealed much about the tasks we face going forward. We might have lost some things, but I would say we gained just as much.]

Subaru: [I can’t argue with that… Though the follow-up with Annerose and Frederica won’t be easy.]

Subaru rubbed his chin, imagining the grief-stricken faces of the two women, and bit his lip.

In the end, they had resolved to frame the situation under the assumption that Clind had been a Witch Cultist.

Clind had shown clear intent to harm Subaru and retaliated without ever attempting to explain himself. With both of these facts in place, not a single person could defend him.

Strictly speaking, Subaru could have defended him, but that was a futile thought.

――Clind’s response to Subaru’s question had become the pivotal moment that sealed his fate.

Echidna: [Irony, isn’t it?. Had he truly been a Witch Cultist, you might have kept him alive to extract information about Gluttony. But…]

Subaru: [But Clind wasn’t a Witch Cultist. Which just made him a dangerous threat.]

And, Subaru was not confident enough to believe he could keep a dangerous man like him alive. At the very least, some kind of safeguard would have been necessary, and Clind had none.

Thus, Annerose and Frederica had been left in tears.

Echidna: [Beyond that, the damage was minimized. You could call it a result worth celebrating.]

Subaru: […Yeah. It’d better be.]

If it were not, then there was no point in making them cry.

He had saved lives. He had prevented further damage. Moreover, he knew it would have been ideal to protect their hearts as well. He knew, but how could he possibly――,

Echidna: [Natsuki Subaru, you must not misunderstand.]

Subaru: [Echidna?]

Echidna: [Your Authority allows you to choose the best result through infinite retries. But, the best result does not necessarily mean saving everyone involved.]

Subaru: [――――]

Subaru fell silent as Echidna’s harsh words sank into his heart.

Even if he chose the best option, it was impossible to prevent everyone he cared for from receiving even the slightest scratch. But, if he accepted that, it felt like Subaru’s entire purpose would crumble.

It did not matter if he had to die a hundred, a thousand, or ten thousand times, so long as he could save everyone else.

That was the only reason Natsuki Subaru continued to challenge the spiral of Death.

Echidna: [Your feelings are noble, and your desire for your loved ones to be at peace is pure.]

Before Subaru, as his resolve wavered, Echidna spoke softly, her voice gentle.

In an uncharacteristically motherly manner, she gazed at Subaru and continued,

Echidna: [But, you cannot protect everyone forever, as if they were in a cradle. Even in the best of outcomes, shards of glass will slip through. And, those shards may cause wounds.]

Subaru: […Then, how do I remove that glass?]

Echidna: [On that matter, my answer is as follows: You cannot remove all of the shards. There will be wounds. However…]

Subaru was about to protest her statement, but Echidna quickly continued, repeating the word “however” in a soft murmur,

Echidna: [So long as you stay alive, your wounds will heal. The Frederica and Annerose who you made cry today, tomorrow….or the day after, will stop crying.]

Subaru: [――――]

Echidna: [Scratches will scab over, and eventually heal. If you worry so much about never even inflicting such wounds, your feet will stop moving, and you will be unable to move forward.]

Echidna’s carefully chosen words pierced Subaru’s heart without mercy.

Indeed, Echidna was right. Subaru had started to lose sight of what “best” even meant. He kept second-guessing himself, even after things had ended.

He had eliminated Clind. And as a result, Annerose and Frederica had been left in tears.

If he did not want to make them cry, should he have let Clind live? But, how else could Subaru have dealt with a man who wanted him dead?

That was――,

Echidna: [――You are not wrong.]

Subaru: [Ah…]

Echidna: [I will continue to guarantee your righteousness. You have saved lives, found a way to the future, and left hope in your wake, thus paving the way for new possibilities. That is the result of your actions.]

Whenever Subaru’s resolve wavered, whenever he felt lost, the Witch was there to bring him back to reason.

Just as she had done countless times before, Echidna stood before Subaru and pulled him back from the brink.

He had used Return by Death hundreds of times now, and as a result, his fear of Death had dulled, and the crushing sense of loss and helplessness had faded. But still, some part of him was wearing thin.

His fraying and fragile soul was pulled back once again, and Subaru held his ground.

A swirl of wrath, calm, fleeting joy; this was what had overwhelmed his heart.

Subaru: [Even if I save them, wounds will remain. But…]

Echidna: [Where there is life…]

Subaru: [Where there is life… there is a future. Where there is a future, there is hope. Where there is hope, there is possibility. Where there is possibility――]

Echidna: [――People can be saved.]

Echidna quietly finished Subaru’s half-mumbled sentence.

People could be saved. The wounds they suffer would eventually heal.

The two who cried today would dry their tears tomorrow, lift their heads the day after, and the next day, perhaps even start walking forward again. Or, at least, begin to prepare to do so.

For that purpose――,

Subaru: [――Let’s move time forward. I… no, we have a lot to do.]

Echidna: [――. Very well. So, where shall we begin next?]

Echidna deliberately did not comment on Subaru’s use of “we.”

And that was fine. He was not looking for her to cling to him.

He just wanted to confirm it: the unbreakable bond of complicity between her and him.

Subaru: [Ah, about that――]

And so, the journey of trial and error would continue, with the Witch bound to him in this shared complicity.
 
